unt":0}}]},{"id":"IC_kwDOAyVBJc5LP0eX","url":"https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/6294#issuecomment-1262438295","body":"The bug still persists","createdAt":"2022-09-29T15:22:51Z","author":{"login":"AndrewLosikhin"},"authorAssociation":"NONE","isMinimized":false,"reactionGroups":[{"content":"THUMBS_UP","reactors":{"totalCount":3}},{"content":"THUMBS_DOWN","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"LAUGH","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"HOORAY","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"CONFUSED","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"HEART","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"ROCKET","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"EYES","reactors":{"totalCount":0}}]},{"id":"IC_kwDOAyVBJc5LVVeo","url":"https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/6294#issuecomment-1263884200","body":"Yes, @AndrewLosikhin. I gave up on that and created a raw query with a subquery that returns only the entity's IDs ordered by the nested field that I wanted with `distinct on` the primary key to avoid duplicated values. That way, I could use the `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` inside my query. After that, I created a new query builder instance calling the `getMany` fn with `whereInIds` just to return the joins data required to build my page with pagination. I also created a separate raw query (based on the first one) to return the total counting. Basically, I did the same behavior from `findManyAndCount`, but manually.

