I have a junction-table-like entity that uses, as a primary key, multiple foreign keys to the same table, without a surrogate primary key:

```typescript
@Entity() class Dependency {
    @PrimaryColumn()
        fromId: number
    @ManyToOne(() => Example, (example: Example) => example.id)
        from: Example

    @PrimaryColumn()
        toId: number
    @ManyToOne(() => Example, (example: Example) => example.id)
        to: Example

    @Column()
        label: string
}

@Entity() class Example {
    @PrimaryColumn()
        id: number

    @Column()
        name: string

    @OneToMany(() => Dependency, (dependency) => dependency.from, { cascade: true, eager: true })
        dependencies: Dependency[]
}
```

When updating such a `Dependency` as a cascade of `save()`ing an `Example`, save() fails to notice that the dependency already exists in the database, and instead tries to insert a new record, causing a unique-key constraint violation:

```typescript
const example1 = new Example()
example1.id = 1
example1.name = "example1"
await manager.save(example1)

const dependency = new Dependency()
dependency.to = example1
dependency.label = "dependency"

const example2 = new Example()
example2.id = 2
example2.name = "example2"
example2.dependencies = [dependency]
await manager.save(example2)

const reloadedExample2 = manager.findOneBy(Example, { id: 2 })
reloadedExample2.dependencies[0].label = "updated-dependency"
await manager.save(example2) // QueryFailedError: duplicate key value violates unique constraint
```

Found on typeorm 0.3.20, with any database driver.","createdAt":"2024-07-01T22:02:24Z","updatedAt":"2025-09-21T20:47:22Z","author":{"login":"redlizard"},"authorAssociation":"NONE","labels":{"nodes":[]},"reactionGroups":[{"content":"THUMBS_UP","reactors":{"totalCount":8}},{"content":"THUMBS_DOWN","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"LAUGH","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"HOORAY","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"CONFUSED","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"HEART","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"ROCKET","reactors":{"totalCount":0}},{"content":"EYES","reactors":{"totalCount":0}}],"comments":{"totalCount":3},"firstComments":{"nodes":[{"id":"IC_kwDOAyVBJc6DMw21","url":"https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/10952#issuecomment-2201161141","body":"### Cause

Investigation shows that when saving the updated `Example`, when checking whether the `Dependency` already exists in the database, the EntityPersistExecutor searches for a `Dependency` with `{ fromId: 2, toId: 2 }`, while the `Dependency` to be inserted has `{ fromId: 2, toId: 1 }`. It searches for the existing entity by replacing all ManyToOne relations pointing back to the `Example` with the ID of the containing `Example`, not just the ManyToOne relation that is the inverse of the OneToMany relation in `Example.dependencies`:

https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/e7649d2746f907ff36b1efb600402dedd5f5a499/src/persistence/Subject.ts#L314-L318

Thus, it fills in both the `fromId` and the `toId` with the ID of the containing `Example`, despite the `dependencies` field being linked only to `Dependency.from`. It then searches for a `Dependency` with `{ fromId: 2, toId: 2 }`, doesn't find it, and then proceeds to queue the creation of the (original, unmodified) `Dependency`, which triggers the unique constraint violation.

To solve this properly, the condition above should not just check that the foreign key entity type matches the type of the containing entity, but also that the relation is the inverse of the relation that is being cascaded, before deciding to fill in the ID of the containing entity.

