When an entity has both `@Column('bigint')` and `@ManyToOne` + `@JoinColumn` mapped to the **same column**, INSERT operations silently lose precision for values exceeding `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`. This is a regression introduced in **0.3.21** and still present in **0.3.28** (latest).

### Expected Behavior

A bigint value like `"3588019939803400076"` (19 digits) should be inserted as-is into the database.

### Actual Behavior

The value is silently converted to a JS `Number`, resulting in `3588019939803400000` being written to the database. No error is thrown — this is **silent data corruption**.

### Steps to reproduce

**Entity setup** (dual mapping on the same bigint column):

```typescript
@Entity('licenses')
export class License {
  @Column('bigint', { primary: true, name: 'id' })
  id: string;

  @Column('bigint', { name: 'source_id', nullable: true })
  sourceId: string | null;

  @ManyToOne(() => Product, (product) => product.licenses)
  @JoinColumn([{ name: 'source_id', referencedColumnName: 'sourceId' }])
  product: Product;
}

@Entity('products')
export class Product {
  @Column('bigint', { name: 'source_id', nullable: true })
  sourceId: string | null;

  @OneToMany(() => License, (license) => license.product)
  licenses: License[];
}
```

### My Environment

| Dependency          | Version  |
| ---                 | ---      |
| Operating System    | Linux (Amazon ECS) / macOS 15.1 |
| Node.js version     | 22.19.0  |
| Typescript version  | 5.3.3    |
| TypeORM version     | 0.3.21 ~ 0.3.28 (all affected). 0.3.19 is safe. |


### Additional Context

PR #10672 (commit `8ffc1b89`) changed `getEntityValueMap()` in `ColumnMetadata.ts` to use a separate code path for single `@JoinColumn` columns:

```typescript
// Before (0.3.19, 0.3.20) — all JoinColumns used the same path:
const map = this.relationMetadata.joinColumns.reduce((map, joinColumn) => {
    const value = joinColumn.referencedColumn!.getEntityValueMap(
        entity[this.relationMetadata!.propertyName],
    )
    if (value === undefined) return map
    return OrmUtils.mergeDeep(map, value)
}, {})

// After (0.3.21+) — single JoinColumn uses getEntityValue() directly:
if (this.relationMetadata.joinColumns.length > 1) {
    // composite key: same as before (safe)
} else {
    const value = this.relationMetadata.joinColumns[0]
        .referencedColumn!.getEntityValue(
            entity[this.relationMetadata!.propertyName],
        )
    if (value) {
        return { [this.propertyName]: value }
    }
}
```

The problem: `getEntityValueMap()` internally calls `createValueMap()`, which has a **bigint-to-string safety conversion**:

```typescript
// In createValueMap() — this protection exists:
if (
    (this.generationStrategy === "increment" || this.generationStrategy === "rowid") &&
    this.type === "bigint" &&
    value !== null
)
    value = String(value)
```

`getEntityValue()` does **not** have this protection. The raw value flows through the persistence pipeline and gets coerced to `Number`, losing precision for values > `2^53 - 1`.

### Relevant Database Driver(s)

- [ ] aurora-mysql
- [x] aurora-postgres
- [ ] better-sqlite3
- [ ] cockroachdb
- [ ] cordova
- [ ] expo
- [ ] mongodb
