From what I'm seeing, bigint JoinColumn precision loss has two distinct root causes. 

In TypeORM, single-`@JoinColumn` relations use [`getEntityValue()`](https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/src/metadata/ColumnMetadata.ts#L709) which lacks the `String(value)` bigint protection that [`createValueMap()`](https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/src/metadata/ColumnMetadata.ts#L578) has — this causes precision loss on any driver returning bigints **as JS Numbers**. 

Confirmed failing on better-sqlite3 which returns bigints as Numbers.

For aurora-postgres scenario, the external `typeorm-aurora-data-api-driver` never sets AWS Data API's `longReturnType: "STRING"` ([AWS SDK #2947](https://github.com/aws/aws-sdk-js/issues/2947)), so generated bigint IDs arrive as lossy JS Numbers before TypeORM even sees them. See driver bundle's [`preparePersistentValue`](https://github.com/ArsenyYankovsky/typeorm-aurora-data-api-driver/blob/master/src/query-transformer/postgres-query-transformer.ts) (no bigint case, falls to default passthrough). 

Standard postgres drivers return bigints as strings natively. 

<details>
<summary>Verification on standard postgres (pg 8.20.0, PostgreSQL 17.9)</summary>

```bash
node -e "
const {DataSource} = require('./build/compiled/src');
(async () => {
  const ds = new DataSource({type:'postgres',host:'localhost',port:5433,username:'username',password:'password',database:'typeorm',synchronize:false});
  await ds.initialize();
  await ds.query('CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bigint_test (val bigint)');
  await ds.query('DELETE FROM bigint_test');
  await ds.query('INSERT INTO bigint_test VALUES (3588019939803400076)');
  const rows = await ds.query('SELECT val FROM bigint_test');
  console.log('value:', rows[0].val);
  console.log('type:', typeof rows[0].val);
  console.log('exact:', rows[0].val === '3588019939803400076');
  await ds.query('DROP TABLE bigint_test');
  await ds.destroy();
})();
"
# value: 3588019939803400076
# type: string
# exact: true
```
</details>

To propose a solution we could maybe do the following:

- The single-JoinColumn path divergence was introduced in [#10672](https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/pull/10672) (composite key fix) — it switched from `getEntityValueMap()` → `createValueMap()` (which has bigint `String()` protection from [#720](https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/720)) to `getEntityValue()` (which doesn't). Restoring consistency here is a minimal fix. (see #12407)
- For aurora-postgres specifically: upstream `typeorm-aurora-data-api-driver` needs `longReturnType: "STRING"` — tracked in [typeorm-aurora-data-api-driver#121](https://github.com/ArsenyYankovsky/typeorm-aurora-data-api-driver/issues/121).
- Longer-term, if drivers move to separate packages, each driver owns the contract of returning bigints as strings — core shouldn't need these safety nets.



