When a query mixes innerJoinAndMapOne with leftJoinAndSelect, 1.1.x emits the SQL joins in a different order than they were added. 0.3.20 preserves call order; 1.1.1 moves the innerJoinAndMapOne join after later leftJoinAndSelect joins.  This changes query plans on PostgreSQL: once a query has more joins than join_collapse_limit (default 8), the planner follows the syntactic join order. Moving a selective INNER JOIN after row-multiplying LEFT JOINs produced a catastrophic plan in production — a query that ran in ~240ms on 0.3.20 took 20–60s on 1.1.1.

### Expected Behavior

Joins in call order:
//   FROM "workout" "w"
//     LEFT JOIN "video" "video" ...
//     INNER JOIN "stats" "stats" ...   <-- 2nd, as called
//     LEFT JOIN "tag" "tags" ...


### Actual Behavior

ACTUAL on 1.1.1 — innerJoinAndMapOne moved after the later leftJoinAndSelect:
//   FROM "workout" "w"
//     LEFT JOIN "video" "video" ...
//     LEFT JOIN "tag" "tags" ...
//     INNER JOIN "stats" "stats" ...   <-- moved to last

### Steps to reproduce

```
// Minimal repro for a TypeORM issue: innerJoinAndMapOne is emitted in a different
// position (relative to leftJoinAndSelect) in 1.1.x vs 0.3.20.
//
//   npm i typeorm@0.3.20 pg && node typeorm_join_order_issue.mjs   # correct (call order)
//   npm i typeorm@1.1.1  pg && node typeorm_join_order_issue.mjs   # reordered
//
// Needs any reachable Postgres (set PG* env or edit below). getSql() only — nothing is executed.

import { DataSource, EntitySchema } from 'typeorm';

const Video = new EntitySchema({
    name: 'Video',
    columns: { id: { type: Number, primary: true } },
    relations: { tags: { type: 'one-to-many', target: 'Tag', inverseSide: 'video' } },
});
const Tag = new EntitySchema({
    name: 'Tag',
    columns: { id: { type: Number, primary: true }, videoId: { type: Number } },
    relations: { video: { type: 'many-to-one', target: 'Video', joinColumn: { name: 'videoId' } } },
});
const Stats = new EntitySchema({ name: 'Stats', columns: { workoutId: { type: Number, primary: true } } });
const Workout = new EntitySchema({
    name: 'Workout',
    columns: { id: { type: Number, primary: true }, videoId: { type: Number } },
    relations: { video: { type: 'many-to-one', target: 'Video', joinColumn: { name: 'videoId' } } },
});

const ds = new DataSource({
    type: 'postgres',
    host: process.env.PGHOST ?? 'localhost',
    port: Number(process.env.PGPORT ?? 5432),
    username: process.env.PGUSER ?? 'postgres',
    password: process.env.PGPASSWORD ?? 'postgres',
    database: process.env.PGDATABASE ?? 'postgres',
    entities: [Workout, Video, Tag, Stats],
    synchronize: false,
});
await ds.initialize();

const sql = ds
    .getRepository('Workout')
    .createQueryBuilder('w')
    .leftJoinAndSelect('w.video', 'video') //                                     called 1st
    .innerJoinAndMapOne('w.stats', 'Stats', 'stats', 'w.id = stats.workoutId') // called 2nd
    .leftJoinAndSelect('video.tags', 'tags') //                                   called 3rd
    .getSql();

console.log(sql.slice(sql.indexOf(' FROM ')).trim());
await ds.destroy();

// EXPECTED (and actual on 0.3.20) — joins in call order:
//   FROM "workout" "w"
//     LEFT JOIN "video" "video" ...
//     INNER JOIN "stats" "stats" ...   <-- 2nd, as called
//     LEFT JOIN "tag" "tags" ...
//
// ACTUAL on 1.1.1 — innerJoinAndMapOne moved after the later leftJoinAndSelect:
//   FROM "workout" "w"
//     LEFT JOIN "video" "video" ...
//     LEFT JOIN "tag" "tags" ...
//     INNER JOIN "stats" "stats" ...   <-- moved to last
//
// Why it matters: once a query has more joins than Postgres `join_collapse_limit`
// (default 8), the planner follows the *syntactic* join order. Moving a selective
// INNER JOIN after row-multiplying LEFT JOINs produced a ~430x slower plan for us
// in production (46s vs 108ms on the same data) because the planner built a huge
// intermediate before filtering.
```

